Seeing a clover means your luck just went up.  It's an extremely good
thing because it means your sacs are now qualified to get an artifact
and you know you are safe to pray.  Janis P points out that it is
possible to get an artifact the sac that would have started the clovers.
Given the chances are like 1-in-20 for the first gift it's bound to
happen in some games - "Use my gift wisely" is a better message than
"You see a four leafed clover at your feet".

Actually, no longer seeing clovers is an even better thing for most
players.  It means your luck has hit maximum.  Before that happens you
need to learn what all of the responses to a sac are.  If they are all
missing then your luck is at max.

And interesting bit of trivia about sacs and artifacts.  When your deity
is angry with you the size of the monster matters.  Corpses too small do
nothing for you.  When your deity is not angry but your prayer timeout
is still positive the size of monsters matters.  Bigger corpses reduce
your prayer timeout more.  When you have seen clovers eqch one gets a
check for an artifact gift.  Strangely, once that's true the size of the
sac no longer matters just the fact that you are doing a sac when not in
trouble.  A newt or a dragon both give the same chance of an artifact
gift at that point.

Yet before the clovers start a next and dragon can have very different
results.  A dragon is big enough to handle anger, but infinite nexts
have no effect on anger (photo-electric effect analogy).  A single
dragon is enough points it might reconcile you in a single sac, while a
next is so few points the fight to kill it brings your prayer timeout
down as much as the sac does.


